Q: Log2 implementation with Cygwin 64-bit seems inefficient A: <p>Noticing that you are actually taking <code>log2</code> of mostly integer values I offer this incompletely finished skeleton implementation of a minimum ripple <code>log(n)</code> polynomial (length 20 coefficients) and a rational approximation (numerator 6, and denominator 6). They are both nominally good to better than double precision but due to rounding of the coefficients to IEEE754 double format and accumulated rounding errors in the computation are not quite there yet.</p>
<p>They would need to be fine tuned by brute force/simulated annealing for precise evaluation to match system <code>log2</code> as closely as possible which can take a lot of time.</p>
<p>One interesting quick observation is that enabling fastmath (slightly dangerous) may gain you some additional speed with the existing code that you already have.</p>
<p>These are the two alternative options. On my machine i5-12600 using MSVC 17.2 the benchmarks are strongly correlated with choice of FP rounding mode. Comment or uncomment lines in the <code>mlog</code> code to try out the different options. The polynomial of length 9 is just slightly better than full float precision minimum ripple ~3.4e-8. Rounding doesn't affect it so much. Something between poly9 and poly 19 or their rational equivalent (which tends to be faster) might well be good enough for your problem.</p>
