<p>The system library <code>log2</code> is good - I have cut a lot of corners in my implementation knowing that the input to the routine will be an unsigned integer. To gain significant speed it will be necessary to accept a less precise answer and/or enable fastmath.</p>
<p>Here is the sample code with a trivial benchmark:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;math.h&gt;
#include &lt;intrin.h&gt;
#include &lt;time.h&gt;

double Ppoly[] = { 1.44269499743435, -7.21340208007326E-01, 4.80692957313851E-01, -3.58408472432460E-01,    2.75641412170034E-01, -1.97118969782254E-01,    1.12856193181675E-01, -4.25670618766400E-02,    7.54918211920329E-03 };
double Ppoly19[] = { 1.44269504088896, -0.7213475204444165197, 0.48089834695413327, -0.36067375974323640,   0.28853899441954325107, -0.24044893089130125268, 0.2060964273520249192997, -0.180312903649444477, 0.1601513448464696526763, -0.143574473163227,  0.12862086124163750545,
                    -0.112927159118275396, 0.09411252690956952, -0.07133280010489688, 0.04683458590259810, -0.025287006735009036420, 0.0105915956335413869694, -0.0031894373321635318714209353, 0.000609490284058726550954471149851, -5.52232505696670679011000e-05 };

double P[] = { 1.44269504088896,    3.53800369442095,   3.13142191702563,   1.21083944242237,   1.95059533866164E-01,   9.84829442488678E-03,   3.30563174479530E-05 };
double Q[] = { 1.00000000000000,    2.95235728559855,   3.31338158239610,   1.76186164161742,   4.49764580795861E-01,   4.89619980843492E-02,   1.57389087413003E-03 };

int NPOL = sizeof(Ppoly) / sizeof(double);
int NPOL19 = sizeof(Ppoly19) / sizeof(double);
int NP = sizeof(P) / sizeof(double);
int NQ = sizeof(Q) / sizeof(double);

double eval_poly(double x, double* p, int n)
{
    double sum = 0;
    for (int i = n; i-- &gt; 0; )
        sum = sum * x + p[i];
    return sum;
}

double mlog2(unsigned long n)
{
    unsigned long lg2n, p2, r2;
//  return log2((double)n);    // system log2 12.7s
    _BitScanReverse(&amp;lg2n, n);
//  return (double)lg2n;       // fastest possible result 1.1s
    p2 = 1ul &lt;&lt; lg2n;
    r2 = (long)n - (long)p2;
    double x = (double)r2 / p2;
//  return lg2n + x * eval_poly(x, Ppoly19, NPOL19);  // 9.4s fastmath
    return lg2n + x * eval_poly(x, P, NP) / eval_poly(x, Q, NQ);  // nominal full double precision 6.54s fastmath
    // above are all nominally full double precision but with rounded coefficients without fine tuning for IEEE754 representation

//  return lg2n + x*eval_poly(x, Ppoly, NPOL);
    // above is nominally just better than full float precision (approx relative error &lt;3.4e-8) rounding of coefficients less of a problem here.

    // finally some really crude fast approximations

    return lg2n + x * (6.03761 + x * (1.05997 - x * 0.09758)) / (4.18582 + x * 2.81418);  // 3,2 relative error optimised 4.8s
    return lg2n + x * (6 + x) / (4.2 + 2.8 * x);         // 2,2 4.7s
    return lg2n + x * (1.4 - 0.64 * x + 0.2 * (x * x));; // poly3 3.7s
}

double slog(unsigned long n)
{
    return log2((double)n);
}

void test(unsigned long x)
{
    double lg2x, mlg2x;
    lg2x = log2(x);
    mlg2x = mlog2(x);
    printf(&quot;%10u  %18.10g %18.10g  %g  %g\n&quot;, x, lg2x, mlg2x, mlg2x - lg2x, 1.0 - mlg2x / lg2x);
}

void time(const char* name, double (*f)(unsigned long), unsigned long low, unsigned long high)
{
    time_t start, end;
    double sum1, sum2, sum3;
    sum1 = sum2 = sum3 = 0;
    start = clock();
    for (unsigned int x = low; x &lt; high; )
    {
        sum1 += (*f)(x++);
        sum2 += (*f)(x++);
        sum3 += (*f)(x++);
    }
    end = clock();
    if (sum1 + sum2 + sum3 == 42) printf(&quot;HHGG\n&quot;);
    else 
        printf(&quot;\nTiming %s Time %i&quot;, name, (int)(end - start));
}

int main()
{
    unsigned int x;
    printf(&quot;\t n          ref log2         mlog2      abs_err     rel_err\n&quot;);
    for (x = 2; x; x += x) // relies on overflow to terminate
    {
        test(x + 1);
        test(x + x / 4);
        test(x + x / 2);
        test(x + 3 * x / 4);
        test(x + x - 1);
        test(x + x);
    }
    time(&quot;Test log2&quot;, mlog2, 1u, 4000000000u);
    time(&quot;System log2&quot;, slog, 1u, 4000000000u);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Beware that benchmarks are artificial and performance on them can be misleading. Even so I think the rational approximation 6,6 is worth a try at almost 2x faster. There isn't much point going shorter than a polynomial of length 9 since function call overheads dominate.</p>
<p>The only way to know for sure how it behaves in the real application is to try it and see. I have known some code that benchmarks very poorly be extremely fast in real world code. The balance of other things being computed at the same time matters a lot.</p>
